<Layout title="Unexpected Application of ML Theory to Language Learning" description="Is this the first time machine learning has transferred to human teaching">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-2">Unexpected Application of ML Theory to Language Learning</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">March 12, 2026</p>
    <p>
      You've probably noticed that computers have learned to talk recently. But plugged-in readers will know that it is only even more recently that we taught computers to spell. Until relatievly recently, LLMs capable of solving complex coding challenges would utterly choke on any question that depended on knowledge of how a word is spelled.
    </p>
    <p>
      This was surprising to a lot of people, because it seems in contradiction with the LLM's ability to respond with perfect spelling. But in-the-know readers will already understand the resolution to the paradox. The LLMs could never spell, because spelling-related tasks are handled by a "dumb" pre- and post-processing step in the pipeline: tokenization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Yes, when you look at a word, you can easily see a sequence of letters, but an LLM cannot, nor does it write words by writing sequences of letters. Instead, 
    </p>
     
    === crap === 

    <p>
      Pedagogy is the study of how to teach effectively. And machine learning is the study of how to effectively teach things to computers. So machine learning could have been named "machine pedagogy" instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Okay, it's not a perfect analogy, because we can modify machines however we want, but we can't modify humans. So the study of machine learning involves how to structure computers so that they learn from their input, as well as how to structure the inputs so they're effective for learning. While human pedagogy can only focus on the latter - how to structure inputs so they're effective for the human learner.
    </p>
    <p>
       Because of the excitement around AI and the relatededness of the problems, it's not surprising that people have tried to take expertise from how to structure inputs to humans, and tried to apply them to computers as well. For example, curriculum learning: people learn better if they're exposed to easy problems before hard problems, which is why a school curriculum is designed to start with easy topics. So it's a natural idea to the order the dataset is sampled for training, to train the model on easy examples first and then gradually increase the difficulty.
    </p>
    <p>
      But I'm not aware of any prior cases of the reverse - where an idea invented to help teach computers is applied directly to the teaching of humas.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
